<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Piast dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 960–<a href="/years/1675">1675</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Piasts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Piast dynasty was the first historical ruling house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, baptised in 966, to the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> in 1370. After 1138 the realm fragmented into duchies held by rival Piast branches in Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a> and elsewhere. The Masovian line died out in 1526 and the last Silesian Piast, George William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a>, in 1675.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty.jpg" alt="Piast dynasty" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Piast_dynasty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under George II the castle was rebuilt in Renaissance style, with a gatehouse adorned with statues of the <b>Piasts</b>, and a gymnasium was founded in 1569.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brzeg">Duchy of Brzeg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cieszyn <b>Piasts</b> ruled for over three centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cieszyn">Duchy of Cieszyn</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Piast</b> lines died out and disputes followed, Głogów was granted to the Jagiellonian Sigismund, who ruled it from 1499 until 1506 before becoming King of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the testament of Boleslaw III in 1138, his son Mieszko III the Old received Greater Poland, with Poznan and Gniezno, the cradle of the <b>Piast</b> state.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li><li><blockquote>They were then reunited under Bolko II the Small, who resisted Bohemian overlordship, ruling as an independent <b>Piast</b> prince while other Silesian dukes became Bohemian vassals.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-jawor">Duchy of Jawor</a></li><li><blockquote>Koźle belonged to the Upper Silesian lands of the Duchy of Bytom, ruled by a branch of the <b>Silesian Piasts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kozle">Duchy of Koźle</a></li></ul></section>
